.TH MARK 1
.SH NAME
mark \- color addressed text in acme's windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mark
[
.B -s
.I style
] [
.B -w
.I id
] [
.I name
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mark
reads addresses, one to a line, and colors the text at each in an
.IR astyles (4)
layer called
.I name
.RB ( mark
by default), in the theme's color
.I style
.RB ( highlight
by default; see
.IR theme (6)).
A line is
.IB file : address\f1,
as
.B grep -n
and the compilers print them,
or an address alone, for the window
.I id
or else
.BR $winid .
The address is
.IR acme (1)'s,
evaluated by the window's
.B addr
file, so
.BR 12 ,
.BR #5,#9
and
.B /regexp/
all work;
what follows it on the line, such as
.BR grep 's
text, is passed over.
A relative
.I file
is taken from the current directory,
and a line whose file has no window is passed over too.
.PP
Each run makes
.IR name 's
layer what its input says:
it replaces the layer in every window it marks
and deletes the layer from every other window that has one,
so an empty input removes it everywhere.
The layer stays after
.I mark
exits, until a run deletes it or its window closes,
and it moves with the window's edits as
.IR astyles (4)
describes.
If the window's body is edited while
.I mark
reads its addresses, that window's marks are dropped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mark every use of
.B Biobuf
in the open C files:
.IP
.EX
grep -n Biobuf *.c /dev/null | mark
.EE
.PP
Mark the compiler's complaints in red, then take them away:
.IP
.EX
mk >[2=1] | mark -s error errors
mark errors </dev/null
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/mark.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR astyles (4),
.IR huh (1),
.IR theme (6)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Mark
prints a line it could not mark, with the line's number, and exits with status
.B errors
if there was one.
